course: dictionary
lesson: 9964
title: Taxman
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tax is money that people pay to the government, and a taxman is the person or office that collects it. In everyday talk, adults often say «the taxman» to mean the tax office, as if it were one person. Income tax is money taken from earnings, which means the money you get from work.
explain: Read this sentence: «Income tax is money taken from earnings for the government; people who earn more pay more, but "half of it goes to the taxman" is usually an exaggeration.» The words around taxman give clues: money, earnings, and government all point to tax being collected.
explain: Exaggeration means making something sound bigger than it really is. So «half of it goes to the taxman» is a figure of speech, not a fact, and the sentence says it is usually an exaggeration. To check, swap in your own meaning: "more than a little of it goes to the tax office" still makes sense, so you have understood it.
fact: Taxman is an informal word, so you hear it in everyday talk more than in formal writing.
fact: «Pay the taxman» means give the money you owe in tax to the government.
fact: «The taxman takes a cut» means the tax office takes a part of your money.
fact: Taxmen is the plural, which means more than one tax collector.
fact: In the UK the tax office is called HMRC, and in the USA it is called the IRS.
exercise: Sam says, «The taxman wants his money by Friday.» Who or what is the taxman? | *The office that collects taxes for the government | A teacher who marks homework | A shop that sells tax forms | A man who sells cars
exercise: «Half of it goes to the taxman» is usually an exaggeration. What does exaggeration mean here? | Asking a question politely | *Saying something bigger than it really is | Counting money very carefully | Saying something exactly right
exercise: «Income tax is money taken from earnings for the government.» Find and copy one word that means money you get from work. | *earnings | government | income | people
test: Tom says, «I owe the taxman a lot this year.» What does owe the taxman suggest? | He has been given a present by the taxman | He has met the taxman in person | *He must pay tax money to the government | He likes the taxman very much
test: Why does the word taxman sound like one person? | It is the name of a famous old king | *Adults speak of the tax office as if it were one person | Taxmen are always named Mr Tax | It means a tree that grows coins
exam: «In old stories the taxman was often disliked.» What does disliked mean? | Given a prize for his work | Loved and admired by everyone | *Not liked, so people were unhappy with him | Hidden away from the village
